                              Quest for the Unicorn
                                  Editing Maps


       The upland and the towns of Quest are drawn from plain text files
     in the 'maps' folder beside the game.  The game reads them when it
     starts, so a change shows the next time you start the game.  The
     dungeons are not in these files; they are generated.

       Keep a copy of the original files before you change them.

     The Map Files
     =============
       The upland is 12 regions in 3 rows of 4.  mapRC.map holds the
     region in row R (1 to 3, north to south) and column C (1 to 4, west
     to east).  Walking off the edge of a region leads into the region
     next to it.

         map11.map    The Northern Realm
         map12.map    The Realm of Nichor
         map13.map    The Olde Realm
         map14.map    The Northeast Wastes
         map21.map    The Great Desert
         map22.map    The Kobold Mountains
         map23.map    The Realm of Marduk
         map24.map    The Realm of Larkon
         map31.map    Radon Valley
         map32.map    The Realm of Talgon
         map33.map    The Enchanted Lands
         map34.map    The Valley of the Unicorns

       The towns:

         radon.map    Radon
         marduk.map   Marduk
         nichor.map   Nichorville
         generic.map  Talfharin, New Solat, Goldville, Capetown,
                      New South Radon and Talgon, which share it
         village.map  Every village
         eshter.map   The Realm of Eshter, the fourth level of the
                      Gateway

     File Format
     ===========
       Each file is 100 lines of exactly 100 characters, and each
     character is one square of the map.  The first character of the
     first line is the north-west corner.  Save the files as plain text.
     Lines may end the Windows way or the Unix way, and a Ctrl+Z at the
     end of the file or empty lines after the last one are ignored.

       If a file is missing, does not have 100 lines, or has a line that
     is not 100 characters long, the game will not start.  It shows a
     message naming the file and the line.

     Characters
     ==========
         Space or .   Plains
         #            Forest
         %            Tree
         ^            Mountains
         v            Mountain floor
         (            Hills
         -            Desert
         =            Swamp
         ~            Water
         |            Dock
         s            Ship
         !            Village
         1            City
         A            Dungeon entrance
         }            Gateway
         @            Brick wall
         +            Stone floor
         ,            Wood floor
         /            Closed door
         \            Open door
         c            Chair
         t            Table
         b            Bed
         m            Shelves
         h            Merchant
         B to Q       Merchant
         Z            Gacha machine

       Any other character is stone floor.

     What the Maps Do Not Change
     ===========================
       Some places belong to the game itself rather than to the maps:

     - A city (1) or a dungeon entrance (A) can only be entered at the
       square where the game expects it.  One drawn anywhere else cannot
       be entered, and painting over one leaves no way in.
     - Every village (!) leads to the same village, so villages work
       wherever they are drawn.
     - You arrive in a town at a fixed square, so keep that square and
       the way from it open.
     - Shops are at fixed squares in each town.  The merchant letters
       (h, B to Q) only draw a merchant: moving one does not move a shop,
       and adding one does not make a new shop.
     - The Gateway in the Valley of the Unicorns and your ship on the
       upland are placed by the game.  A } in a map draws a closed
       Gateway that leads nowhere.

       The game redraws the upland and the towns from the maps each time
     you enter them, so a change also shows in games saved before it.
